Existing tests encode the current behavior and pass (`./node_modules/.bin/jest test/datetime/tokenParse.test.js` → 46 passed): test/datetime/tokenParse.test.js:597-604 "explains a bad match" asserts `ex.rawMatches` is `null`, `ex.matches` is an Object with 0 keys, and `ex.result` is an Object with 0 keys — i.e. it documents `result === {}` rather than `null` on failure.

**Summary:** `match` returns `[null, {}]` when the input does not match the regex (raw matches `null`, token map an empty but truthy object), and `[Array, {token: value}]` when it does. Only `explainFromTokens` calls it; because the empty object is truthy, the `matches ? … : [null, null]` fallback at line 391 is dead code on the no-match path and `result` ends up `{}` instead of `null`.

